    {
      "filename": "Cobh (pronounced Cove) dominates Cork Harbour one of the largest natural harbours in the world (7174108319).jpg",
      "title": "File:Cobh (pronounced Cove) dominates Cork Harbour one of the largest natural harbours in the world (7174108319).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/13/Cobh_%28pronounced_Cove%29_dominates_Cork_Harbour_one_of_the_largest_natural_harbours_in_the_world_%287174108319%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cobh_(pronounced_Cove)_dominates_Cork_Harbour_one_of_the_largest_natural_harbours_in_the_world_(7174108319).jpg",
      "width": 6000,
      "height": 4000,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "William Murphy from Dublin, Ireland",
      "description": "Cobh  (pronounced Cove) dominates Cork Harbour one of the largest natural harbours in the world. This colourful town has experienced invasion, emigration, transportation and a wealth of maritime adventure.\nAttractions in the town include St. Colman's Cathedral, a magnificent 19th century Gothic revival and home to the largest Carillon (49-bells), in Ireland and the UK. The Cathedral Spire overlooks the brightly painted town of Cobh and the inner islands of the harbour, Spike Island, renowned for its historical legacy and Fort and Haulbowline Island site of the oldest yacht club in the world, the Royal Cork Yacht Club which was founded in 1720.\nCobh  became the country's main emigration port following the Great Famine of 1846 - 1848 Following the visit of Queen Victoria in 1849, the town was renamed Queenstown but in 1921, it reverted to the old name of Cobh.\n\nOn the shoreline, a statue memorialises Annie Moore, the first immigrant to disembark at Ellis Island. Coffin and prisoner ships, bound for the southern hemisphere, all embarked from this shoreline. The Titanic's last port of call was Cobh and the ill-fated Lusitania also has a tragic association with the town. Cobh's unique origins are dramatically recalled in the Queenstown Story, on the Titanic Trail and at the Cobh Museum. The Cobh Heritage Centre also offers a Genealogy service",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 6 June 2012, 10:32",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "cobh-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gc4n",
      "mediumPath": "_m/g/c/4/n/cobh-wp/cobh-pronounced-cove-dominates-medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/g/c/4/n/cobh-wp/cobh-pronounced-cove-dominates-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Cobh (pronounced Cove) dominates Cork Harbour one of the largest natural harbours in the world (7174119723).jpg",
      "title": "File:Cobh (pronounced Cove) dominates Cork Harbour one of the largest natural harbours in the world (7174119723).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4d/Cobh_%28pronounced_Cove%29_dominates_Cork_Harbour_one_of_the_largest_natural_harbours_in_the_world_%287174119723%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cobh_(pronounced_Cove)_dominates_Cork_Harbour_one_of_the_largest_natural_harbours_in_the_world_(7174119723).jpg",
      "width": 6000,
      "height": 4000,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "William Murphy from Dublin, Ireland",
      "description": "The picturesque seaport town of Cobh is located on the Great Island in Cork Harbour, one of the largest natural harbours in the world. Cobh has a very long and interesting history. Being a major transatlantic port, Cobh was the departure point for many Irish emigrants to North America, and was the final port of call for the RMS Titanic.",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 6 June 2012, 10:34",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "cobh-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gc4n",
      "mediumPath": "_m/g/c/4/n/cobh-wp/cobh-pronounced-cove-dominates-1-medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/g/c/4/n/cobh-wp/cobh-pronounced-cove-dominates-1-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Cobh (pronounced Cove) \"Cobh Dry Cleaners\" (7174125569).jpg",
      "title": "File:Cobh (pronounced Cove) \"Cobh Dry Cleaners\" (7174125569).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a6/Cobh_%28pronounced_Cove%29_%22Cobh_Dry_Cleaners%22_%287174125569%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cobh_(pronounced_Cove)_%22Cobh_Dry_Cleaners%22_(7174125569).jpg",
      "width": 6000,
      "height": 4000,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "William Murphy from Dublin, Ireland",
      "description": "The Napoleonic Wars meant the town became a British Naval port with its own admiral and much of the present day buildings were built. The cessation of hostilities dented its prosperity for a time but it became widely known as a health resort and many convalescents came to Cove to avail of its temperate climate. Amongst these was Rev. Charles Wolfe who wrote \"The Burial of Sir John Moore After Corunna\" and whose remains are buried in the Old Church Cemetery outside the town.",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 6 June 2012, 10:35",
